WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.910
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:06.010 --> 00:00:07.510
KATHARINA PROBST:
We're here to talk

00:00:07.510 --> 00:00:14.140
about cost-effective cluster
sharing with GKE multi-tenancy.

00:00:14.140 --> 00:00:16.239
If you listened to
yesterday's keynote,

00:00:16.239 --> 00:00:18.850
you heard [INAUDIBLE]
talk about how

00:00:18.850 --> 00:00:21.190
it is very important
for our customers

00:00:21.190 --> 00:00:24.610
to deliver value to their
own customers quickly

00:00:24.610 --> 00:00:26.860
and to be able to
iterate quickly.

00:00:26.860 --> 00:00:29.680
I'm sure this is very important
to all of you as well.

00:00:29.680 --> 00:00:34.300
And of course, our
customers and you as well

00:00:34.300 --> 00:00:38.170
want to deliver value to your
customer at the minimum cost

00:00:38.170 --> 00:00:39.500
possible.

00:00:39.500 --> 00:00:43.540
So we're here to talk about how
a GKE multi-tenancy strategy

00:00:43.540 --> 00:00:46.600
fits into this picture.

00:00:46.600 --> 00:00:48.307
My name is Katharina Probst.

00:00:48.307 --> 00:00:50.890
I work at Google, and I'm going
to spend a few minutes talking

00:00:50.890 --> 00:00:54.220
about why GKE multi-tenancy
is something that you

00:00:54.220 --> 00:00:56.570
might want to consider.

00:00:56.570 --> 00:00:58.720
Shahid Masud will talk
about the what and the

00:00:58.720 --> 00:01:00.430
how of multi-tenancy.

00:01:00.430 --> 00:01:03.700
And then finally, Karl
Isenberg from Cruise Automation

00:01:03.700 --> 00:01:05.920
will give us a
customer perspective,

00:01:05.920 --> 00:01:12.520
a real world scale use case
of multi-tenancy in practice.

00:01:12.520 --> 00:01:14.640
So let's spend just a
couple of minutes talking

00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:18.860
about why multi-tenancy.

00:01:18.860 --> 00:01:22.550
At a glance,
Kubernetes is a user

00:01:22.550 --> 00:01:26.270
or a set of users
interacting with a master.

00:01:26.270 --> 00:01:29.660
Master holds the API server,
the scheduler, other things.

00:01:29.660 --> 00:01:33.230
And the master is
responsible for managing

00:01:33.230 --> 00:01:37.230
a set of nodes that run
your actual application.

00:01:37.230 --> 00:01:38.150
All right?

00:01:38.150 --> 00:01:43.160
So when you have one
cluster and one user,

00:01:43.160 --> 00:01:45.830
things are relatively
straightforward, right?

00:01:45.830 --> 00:01:47.550
User interacts with the master.

00:01:47.550 --> 00:01:51.750
A master manages the nodes.

00:01:51.750 --> 00:01:54.620
But what happens when
you have multiple users

00:01:54.620 --> 00:01:57.180
in multiple clusters?

00:01:57.180 --> 00:01:59.150
So think, for
instance, if you have

00:01:59.150 --> 00:02:01.580
many teams in your
own organization that

00:02:01.580 --> 00:02:04.220
have to run different
applications

00:02:04.220 --> 00:02:08.759
or even external customers that
run different applications.

00:02:08.759 --> 00:02:13.100
So what happens when you set
up a separate Kubernetes stack

00:02:13.100 --> 00:02:16.105
for each one of your users?

00:02:16.105 --> 00:02:17.480
And I want to give
you two things

00:02:17.480 --> 00:02:20.630
to think about, going back
to our cost and velocity

00:02:20.630 --> 00:02:22.280
arguments.

00:02:22.280 --> 00:02:24.710
So think about how this
scales financially.

00:02:24.710 --> 00:02:28.770
Having a completely separate
stack, if you look at this,

00:02:28.770 --> 00:02:30.680
one of the things
you will notice

00:02:30.680 --> 00:02:33.980
is that you now
have a proliferation

00:02:33.980 --> 00:02:35.870
of these clusters, right?

00:02:35.870 --> 00:02:40.970
And one of the things that you
now don't have the easy ability

00:02:40.970 --> 00:02:43.880
to do anymore is
to share resources

00:02:43.880 --> 00:02:45.800
among these applications.

00:02:45.800 --> 00:02:47.930
This is something that
we hear from customers

00:02:47.930 --> 00:02:51.290
and that also gels
with my own experience.

00:02:51.290 --> 00:02:52.850
Oftentimes, what
you have is you have

00:02:52.850 --> 00:02:55.980
applications that run at
different times of the day.

00:02:55.980 --> 00:02:57.650
So you might have
some applications that

00:02:57.650 --> 00:03:00.202
take a lot of user
traffic during the day,

00:03:00.202 --> 00:03:02.660
and then you might have some
applications that you can run,

00:03:02.660 --> 00:03:06.170
for instance, batch applications
that you can run at night.

00:03:06.170 --> 00:03:11.060
And you want to have the ability
to use one set of resources

00:03:11.060 --> 00:03:12.695
to run both of these easily.

00:03:15.310 --> 00:03:17.350
Another thing to
think about is how

00:03:17.350 --> 00:03:21.262
does this proliferation of
clusters scale operationally?

00:03:21.262 --> 00:03:23.470
This is also something that's
very close to my heart.

00:03:23.470 --> 00:03:26.920
If you have a lot of
different clusters running,

00:03:26.920 --> 00:03:30.640
now you have tens, or hundreds,
or thousands of dashboards

00:03:30.640 --> 00:03:33.280
that you need to
understand and manage.

00:03:33.280 --> 00:03:37.210
Now you have to figure out how
to apply policies across all

00:03:37.210 --> 00:03:39.070
of those consistently.

00:03:39.070 --> 00:03:43.000
You have to figure out how to
apply, for instance, networking

00:03:43.000 --> 00:03:46.670
or other kinds of setups
consistently across all

00:03:46.670 --> 00:03:47.170
of them.

00:03:47.170 --> 00:03:50.270
How do you roll out new
versions, and so forth?

00:03:50.270 --> 00:03:52.240
So those are the
kinds of things where

00:03:52.240 --> 00:03:54.730
if you reach a certain
scale, it becomes

00:03:54.730 --> 00:03:58.730
more difficult to manage
all of these clusters.

00:03:58.730 --> 00:04:01.000
Now an alternative
to this approach

00:04:01.000 --> 00:04:03.580
of setting up a separate
cluster for each app

00:04:03.580 --> 00:04:08.440
is to have a shared cluster,
so a multi-tenant cluster.

00:04:08.440 --> 00:04:10.570
And the way this
will work, generally,

00:04:10.570 --> 00:04:14.050
is that you have a large cluster
with a large set of nodes.

00:04:14.050 --> 00:04:16.810
And you split it
up by namespace,

00:04:16.810 --> 00:04:20.829
and assign an application
to a namespace.

00:04:20.829 --> 00:04:22.810
And now you can
have multiple users

00:04:22.810 --> 00:04:26.005
that own different applications
running in the same cluster.

00:04:29.690 --> 00:04:31.520
One pattern that we've seen--

00:04:31.520 --> 00:04:34.580
and that Karl will talk
about in greater detail--

00:04:34.580 --> 00:04:38.810
is different teams having their
different applications that

00:04:38.810 --> 00:04:41.510
run in different
namespaces, as I just said,

00:04:41.510 --> 00:04:45.980
on a shared Kubernetes cluster
or set of Kubernetes clusters.

00:04:45.980 --> 00:04:47.720
And then that also
gives you the ability

00:04:47.720 --> 00:04:50.630
to build platforms
on top of that.

00:04:50.630 --> 00:04:52.970
That, again, gets
you to this place

00:04:52.970 --> 00:04:55.310
where you can be far more
consistent across all

00:04:55.310 --> 00:04:59.400
of the apps that are running
on these shared clusters.

00:04:59.400 --> 00:05:02.780
Now with that, I will hand it
off to my colleague, Shahid,

00:05:02.780 --> 00:05:07.240
and he will talk about the what
of multi-tenancy and the how.

00:05:07.240 --> 00:05:08.240
SHAHID MASUD: All right.

00:05:08.240 --> 00:05:10.880
So first, let's try
to be on the same page

00:05:10.880 --> 00:05:13.790
in terms of what
multi-tenancy means.

00:05:13.790 --> 00:05:16.520
In our context, we're
looking at features

00:05:16.520 --> 00:05:18.470
that are part of
Kubernetes Engine that

00:05:18.470 --> 00:05:19.730
support multi-tenancy.

00:05:19.730 --> 00:05:21.980
And from the Kubernetes
Engine point of view,

00:05:21.980 --> 00:05:24.260
the things that we
want to be able to do

00:05:24.260 --> 00:05:27.740
are to provide isolation
and fair resource

00:05:27.740 --> 00:05:30.380
sharing for the
users of a cluster

00:05:30.380 --> 00:05:31.730
within one single cluster.

00:05:31.730 --> 00:05:34.370
So we want to make sure
that the users aren't

00:05:34.370 --> 00:05:37.790
able to hog resources or
step on each other's doors

00:05:37.790 --> 00:05:42.510
as they're sharing one cluster
in a multi-tenant environment.

00:05:42.510 --> 00:05:46.880
The other thing to understand
is what your tenants are like.

00:05:46.880 --> 00:05:50.690
So you can think of your tenants
as whether you trust them,

00:05:50.690 --> 00:05:53.760
whether you somewhat trust them,
or whether they're untrusted.

00:05:53.760 --> 00:05:56.690
So a trusted tenant
would be teams

00:05:56.690 --> 00:06:00.050
within one company, where
you know what kind of code

00:06:00.050 --> 00:06:01.460
they're deploying.

00:06:01.460 --> 00:06:04.940
You may have some audit
processes or some other checks

00:06:04.940 --> 00:06:08.630
and balances that make sure that
only certain type of components

00:06:08.630 --> 00:06:10.560
get deployed in your cluster.

00:06:10.560 --> 00:06:14.720
So that's what I mean
by a trusted tenant.

00:06:14.720 --> 00:06:18.480
You may not have as rigorous
audit procedures in place.

00:06:18.480 --> 00:06:20.750
And the tenants, you
still trust them,

00:06:20.750 --> 00:06:24.150
but they can deploy any sort
of code in the cluster as well.

00:06:24.150 --> 00:06:25.940
And on the other
extreme, you have

00:06:25.940 --> 00:06:29.630
non-trusted users or tenants
who can deploy anything.

00:06:29.630 --> 00:06:31.940
Basically, you're
providing a platform

00:06:31.940 --> 00:06:33.860
where users bring their code.

00:06:33.860 --> 00:06:37.010
And they run that
code on your platform.

00:06:37.010 --> 00:06:40.370
So if you have the
non-trusted tenants,

00:06:40.370 --> 00:06:43.070
you may want to look
at this new feature

00:06:43.070 --> 00:06:47.330
with GKE called GKE
Sandbox, which uses gVisor

00:06:47.330 --> 00:06:49.970
to add an extra
layer of security

00:06:49.970 --> 00:06:52.220
between the container
and the infrastructure.

00:06:52.220 --> 00:06:55.670
So it provides defense
in-depth capabilities

00:06:55.670 --> 00:06:59.490
without requiring much
change in the application.

00:06:59.490 --> 00:07:02.660
So you may want to see
this session, which

00:07:02.660 --> 00:07:05.720
we will talk more in
detail about multi-tendency

00:07:05.720 --> 00:07:08.420
using Sandbox.

00:07:08.420 --> 00:07:10.670
Back to this session,
we are focusing mainly

00:07:10.670 --> 00:07:12.980
on the use case, where
you trust the tenants

00:07:12.980 --> 00:07:15.050
and you know the kind
of code that they're

00:07:15.050 --> 00:07:17.025
deploying in the cluster.

00:07:17.025 --> 00:07:18.650
So with that out of
the way, let's look

00:07:18.650 --> 00:07:21.710
at how you might
implement multi-tenancy.

00:07:21.710 --> 00:07:23.270
There are four
things that you want

00:07:23.270 --> 00:07:27.380
to be careful of when you're
setting up multi-tenancy.

00:07:27.380 --> 00:07:29.780
You want to set up the
right access control.

00:07:29.780 --> 00:07:33.590
You want to set up the right
way of resource sharing,

00:07:33.590 --> 00:07:35.390
a fair way of resource sharing.

00:07:35.390 --> 00:07:40.880
You want to be able to isolate
certain users and workloads

00:07:40.880 --> 00:07:43.400
from other workloads and users.

00:07:43.400 --> 00:07:46.650
And then you finally want to
see what your users are actually

00:07:46.650 --> 00:07:47.150
doing.

00:07:47.150 --> 00:07:50.420
You want to see how much
resources they're using

00:07:50.420 --> 00:07:52.050
and so on and so forth.

00:07:52.050 --> 00:07:54.240
So let's look at each in detail.

00:07:54.240 --> 00:07:56.330
First, let's look
at access control.

00:07:56.330 --> 00:07:59.480
So GCP at a higher level
provides a mechanism

00:07:59.480 --> 00:08:02.990
of access control using
Projects and Identity and Access

00:08:02.990 --> 00:08:04.230
Management.

00:08:04.230 --> 00:08:07.160
So Project is a high
level primitive,

00:08:07.160 --> 00:08:10.020
which allows you to
sort of group resources

00:08:10.020 --> 00:08:14.660
and then allows you to
provide rules to users within,

00:08:14.660 --> 00:08:16.680
for those resources.

00:08:16.680 --> 00:08:19.190
So one key takeaway
here is that when

00:08:19.190 --> 00:08:23.450
you add a rule for a
user for a project,

00:08:23.450 --> 00:08:26.370
it applies to all the
clusters in that user.

00:08:26.370 --> 00:08:29.210
So if you give someone the
Kubernetes Engine developer

00:08:29.210 --> 00:08:32.929
role for a project, they'll have
that role for any clusters that

00:08:32.929 --> 00:08:34.490
are contained in that project.

00:08:34.490 --> 00:08:37.429
So for that reason, one
thing that we recommend

00:08:37.429 --> 00:08:40.039
to our customers is to
separate your environments

00:08:40.039 --> 00:08:41.610
at least in different projects.

00:08:41.610 --> 00:08:44.840
So your production environment
should be one project,

00:08:44.840 --> 00:08:48.240
and your nonprod could be
another project as well.

00:08:48.240 --> 00:08:51.720
So that way, if you give
someone access to the nonprod,

00:08:51.720 --> 00:08:54.020
they don't automatically
have access to the production

00:08:54.020 --> 00:08:55.660
environment as well.

00:08:55.660 --> 00:08:59.060
So that's one key
point to remember.

00:08:59.060 --> 00:09:01.940
And then within
the clusters, you

00:09:01.940 --> 00:09:04.070
have the boundary of
namespaces that you

00:09:04.070 --> 00:09:06.500
can use to separate
out different users

00:09:06.500 --> 00:09:08.640
and tenants within the cluster.

00:09:08.640 --> 00:09:11.960
So next, let's look
at how the users

00:09:11.960 --> 00:09:16.020
are authenticated and authorized
to use resources in a cluster.

00:09:16.020 --> 00:09:17.340
So it's a three-step process.

00:09:17.340 --> 00:09:19.350
There's authentication,
authorization,

00:09:19.350 --> 00:09:21.110
and then there's
admission control.

00:09:21.110 --> 00:09:25.800
The authentication is provided
by the IAM that we saw earlier.

00:09:25.800 --> 00:09:28.130
So the rules that the
users have in the project

00:09:28.130 --> 00:09:31.370
would determine whether they
can access this cluster or not.

00:09:31.370 --> 00:09:34.750
And at a minimum, you
need the get permission

00:09:34.750 --> 00:09:41.740
on the container API of GKE to
be able to access this cluster.

00:09:41.740 --> 00:09:44.380
And then it's up to the
role based access control

00:09:44.380 --> 00:09:46.840
that are a part
of Kubernetes that

00:09:46.840 --> 00:09:49.880
determine what that user
can do in the cluster.

00:09:49.880 --> 00:09:52.150
So you want to set
up the right roles

00:09:52.150 --> 00:09:55.880
and bind those roles
to those users.

00:09:55.880 --> 00:09:59.410
So the roles come in two
flavors in Kubernetes.

00:09:59.410 --> 00:10:02.170
One are cluster roles,
which are cluster wide.

00:10:02.170 --> 00:10:05.230
So you want to be careful as
to who you give those roles

00:10:05.230 --> 00:10:09.040
because these cluster
roles go across namespaces.

00:10:09.040 --> 00:10:11.050
And since you're
limiting your tenants

00:10:11.050 --> 00:10:14.110
to individual namespaces,
you're most likely

00:10:14.110 --> 00:10:17.980
going to need to create the
roles for each namespace

00:10:17.980 --> 00:10:22.240
and then give those roles
to the appropriate users

00:10:22.240 --> 00:10:25.720
of that namespace.

00:10:25.720 --> 00:10:28.960
And you can also--

00:10:28.960 --> 00:10:32.930
you can bind these rules
with Google Groups as well.

00:10:32.930 --> 00:10:35.080
This is a new beta
feature that has come out.

00:10:35.080 --> 00:10:38.470
A lot of our customers were
requesting this feature,

00:10:38.470 --> 00:10:41.620
so that they can assign
the roles or cluster

00:10:41.620 --> 00:10:44.710
roles to Google Groups
and then manage users

00:10:44.710 --> 00:10:47.540
by adding and removing
them from those groups.

00:10:47.540 --> 00:10:50.710
Then the next step in the
process is admission control.

00:10:50.710 --> 00:10:54.990
So before Kubernetes
resources persisted,

00:10:54.990 --> 00:10:59.560
Kubernetes makes sure that it
meets your admission criteria.

00:10:59.560 --> 00:11:01.060
And this is an
opportunity for you

00:11:01.060 --> 00:11:04.570
to modify that and allow and
disallow certain objects.

00:11:04.570 --> 00:11:08.470
Or you can change those objects
to add some default values

00:11:08.470 --> 00:11:11.770
or change them to
meet your policy.

00:11:11.770 --> 00:11:15.040
So that's another mechanism
that's available to you

00:11:15.040 --> 00:11:16.750
to do that.

00:11:16.750 --> 00:11:19.300
One more feature-- so
those are for the users.

00:11:19.300 --> 00:11:22.270
How about the workloads that
are running in your system?

00:11:22.270 --> 00:11:25.420
So this is another area
where our customers

00:11:25.420 --> 00:11:28.870
have to do some manual
workarounds to get past that.

00:11:28.870 --> 00:11:31.640
We have a new feature,
which is hitting beta soon,

00:11:31.640 --> 00:11:33.490
called Workload Identity.

00:11:33.490 --> 00:11:37.180
What it allows you to do
is use GCP service accounts

00:11:37.180 --> 00:11:40.720
to give your workloads
access to GCPD resources

00:11:40.720 --> 00:11:43.000
without you having
to manage the keys

00:11:43.000 --> 00:11:45.430
or providing these
keys to the pods.

00:11:45.430 --> 00:11:47.660
GCP will take care
of that for you.

00:11:47.660 --> 00:11:50.050
And this resolves a
number of workarounds

00:11:50.050 --> 00:11:52.930
that our customers had
to implement in the past.

00:11:52.930 --> 00:11:57.010
And typically, you
either use the identity

00:11:57.010 --> 00:11:59.500
of the node where the
workload is running

00:11:59.500 --> 00:12:01.030
to access GCP resources.

00:12:01.030 --> 00:12:03.370
But you can imagine in a
multi-tenant environment,

00:12:03.370 --> 00:12:06.010
that's not desirable
because the same node can

00:12:06.010 --> 00:12:11.180
be hosting different types of
workloads for different users.

00:12:11.180 --> 00:12:12.650
So you don't want to do that.

00:12:12.650 --> 00:12:15.400
And it also takes
the burden away

00:12:15.400 --> 00:12:18.970
from the administrators of
either downloading the key,

00:12:18.970 --> 00:12:21.550
and then manually managing
that key, and providing that

00:12:21.550 --> 00:12:23.920
to the workloads that they want.

00:12:23.920 --> 00:12:28.270
So that's a great new feature
that supports multi-tenancy.

00:12:28.270 --> 00:12:31.000
And there's a detailed
talk on that feature

00:12:31.000 --> 00:12:34.510
also that you may want
to look at to understand

00:12:34.510 --> 00:12:37.010
that feature in more detail.

00:12:37.010 --> 00:12:39.320
So next, let's look
at resource sharing.

00:12:39.320 --> 00:12:42.287
Kubernetes, as I said,
has a lot of sort

00:12:42.287 --> 00:12:46.030
of mechanisms built into
it to allow you to do that.

00:12:46.030 --> 00:12:50.410
You can define quotas, which is
the aggregate sort of resources

00:12:50.410 --> 00:12:52.360
that a namespace can have.

00:12:52.360 --> 00:12:57.790
You can add default values for
resource limits and requests

00:12:57.790 --> 00:13:01.120
that the users can make
for their workloads.

00:13:01.120 --> 00:13:04.960
You can also use pod
affinity and anti-affinity

00:13:04.960 --> 00:13:09.010
to sort of split the workloads
across different nodes

00:13:09.010 --> 00:13:12.910
and sort of isolate
them from each other.

00:13:12.910 --> 00:13:16.420
And then pod
priority can be used

00:13:16.420 --> 00:13:20.420
to sort of prioritize different
workloads over the other.

00:13:20.420 --> 00:13:23.920
So high priority workloads
can preempt lower priority

00:13:23.920 --> 00:13:26.420
workloads as well.

00:13:26.420 --> 00:13:28.840
Next, let's look at
runtime isolation.

00:13:28.840 --> 00:13:30.480
Again, there are a
number of features.

00:13:30.480 --> 00:13:32.830
I already mentioned
Sandbox pods that you

00:13:32.830 --> 00:13:36.910
can use to provide an
extra layer of security

00:13:36.910 --> 00:13:41.740
between the containers and audit
what the containers can do.

00:13:41.740 --> 00:13:45.400
You can also set up the pod
security policies and security

00:13:45.400 --> 00:13:51.430
contacts, which allow you to
limit what resources the pod

00:13:51.430 --> 00:13:54.040
can access, whether it can
access the file system,

00:13:54.040 --> 00:13:56.170
or it can run in
the host network,

00:13:56.170 --> 00:13:57.860
and so on and so forth.

00:13:57.860 --> 00:13:59.740
And network policy
also allows you

00:13:59.740 --> 00:14:03.130
to determine which pods
are able to communicate

00:14:03.130 --> 00:14:07.090
with one another, what
sort of network access

00:14:07.090 --> 00:14:09.010
those pods will allow.

00:14:09.010 --> 00:14:10.510
So these are sort
of primitives that

00:14:10.510 --> 00:14:14.500
are built into either Kubernetes
or the Kubernetes Engine

00:14:14.500 --> 00:14:15.700
that you can use.

00:14:15.700 --> 00:14:19.630
One challenge that this poses
is there's a lot of policies

00:14:19.630 --> 00:14:21.290
that you have to manage.

00:14:21.290 --> 00:14:25.480
How do you apply those policies
across multiple clusters

00:14:25.480 --> 00:14:27.100
that you might
have and namespaces

00:14:27.100 --> 00:14:28.560
within those clusters.

00:14:28.560 --> 00:14:33.690
And also, how do you ensure
that the policies that you

00:14:33.690 --> 00:14:35.220
want applied don't drift?

00:14:35.220 --> 00:14:37.740
People don't come in
and change the policy,

00:14:37.740 --> 00:14:41.620
and you want to have better
control on that policy.

00:14:41.620 --> 00:14:44.880
And that's where a new
feature called Anthos Config

00:14:44.880 --> 00:14:46.230
Management comes in.

00:14:46.230 --> 00:14:50.120
How many of you watched
the keynote yesterday?

00:14:50.120 --> 00:14:54.120
So the keen observers may
have seen the Anthos Config

00:14:54.120 --> 00:14:56.370
Management being
used during the demo

00:14:56.370 --> 00:15:00.270
that they showed for Anthos to
apply the different policies

00:15:00.270 --> 00:15:02.460
or changes to your cluster.

00:15:02.460 --> 00:15:06.960
What it allows you to do
is to define your policy

00:15:06.960 --> 00:15:08.220
in a center place.

00:15:08.220 --> 00:15:11.040
You can check that into a
repo, so you can version

00:15:11.040 --> 00:15:12.340
control that.

00:15:12.340 --> 00:15:16.710
And then Config Management will
automatically pull that policy

00:15:16.710 --> 00:15:19.710
and apply it to all the
clusters that are enrolled

00:15:19.710 --> 00:15:22.110
in that Config Management.

00:15:22.110 --> 00:15:26.650
So I'll give you a brief demo
of that to show how that works.

00:15:26.650 --> 00:15:30.690
So I have a cluster where
I don't have any workloads

00:15:30.690 --> 00:15:32.110
running right now.

00:15:32.110 --> 00:15:36.780
And I have defined my policy
into this repo that I have.

00:15:36.780 --> 00:15:40.000
And you can define any
Kubernetes native resource.

00:15:40.000 --> 00:15:42.330
It doesn't have to be
just those policies.

00:15:42.330 --> 00:15:44.880
You can define the quota.

00:15:44.880 --> 00:15:47.190
Here, I have a
quota defined, where

00:15:47.190 --> 00:15:51.480
I can have 70 pods in
each namespace and so on.

00:15:51.480 --> 00:15:54.070
And I can also
define deployment.

00:15:54.070 --> 00:15:57.180
So if you wanted to have
some workloads running

00:15:57.180 --> 00:16:00.960
by default in those namespaces,
you can define that as well.

00:16:00.960 --> 00:16:06.090
So I have this workload that
I'm running in this namespace

00:16:06.090 --> 00:16:07.120
as well.

00:16:07.120 --> 00:16:10.500
So next, what I'll
do is I will apply

00:16:10.500 --> 00:16:12.910
that policy in that cluster.

00:16:12.910 --> 00:16:16.530
So I have the config management
operator already running

00:16:16.530 --> 00:16:17.710
in the cluster.

00:16:17.710 --> 00:16:19.890
And this is the
operator that's looking

00:16:19.890 --> 00:16:25.560
at custom resources that
define where your policy should

00:16:25.560 --> 00:16:28.770
be pulled from.

00:16:28.770 --> 00:16:33.330
So the way you do that is
you define a custom resource

00:16:33.330 --> 00:16:35.130
of type config management.

00:16:35.130 --> 00:16:38.340
And you pointed to the repo
where your policy exists.

00:16:38.340 --> 00:16:40.920
So in this case, I'm
pointing it to the repo

00:16:40.920 --> 00:16:42.180
that I just showed you.

00:16:42.180 --> 00:16:45.653
And I can specify which branch
it should pull the policy from

00:16:45.653 --> 00:16:46.820
and apply it to the cluster.

00:16:46.820 --> 00:16:48.570
So I'm using master.

00:16:48.570 --> 00:16:52.680
Depending on your own
sort of branching policy,

00:16:52.680 --> 00:16:55.000
you can choose the
branch that you want.

00:16:55.000 --> 00:17:00.360
So what I'll do next is
I will apply this policy

00:17:00.360 --> 00:17:01.665
into my cluster.

00:17:09.130 --> 00:17:11.819
And what that is doing is--

00:17:11.819 --> 00:17:14.160
essentially, what
this will do is

00:17:14.160 --> 00:17:17.319
it will create that
policy in the cluster.

00:17:17.319 --> 00:17:20.920
So one thing you'll notice is
if I go back to the workloads,

00:17:20.920 --> 00:17:23.670
it will start a
few processes that

00:17:23.670 --> 00:17:28.440
will start pulling the
policy from the git repo

00:17:28.440 --> 00:17:32.980
and start applying it
in your cluster as well.

00:17:32.980 --> 00:17:37.800
So once this policy has been
applied, what you will notice

00:17:37.800 --> 00:17:40.860
is that anything that
I've defined in this repo

00:17:40.860 --> 00:17:43.800
will be applied to that cluster.

00:17:43.800 --> 00:17:47.130
And the namespaces that I've
defined-- blue, and green,

00:17:47.130 --> 00:17:48.060
and carmine--

00:17:48.060 --> 00:17:51.090
and the quota that I've defined
will be applied to that cluster

00:17:51.090 --> 00:17:53.470
as well.

00:17:53.470 --> 00:17:58.200
So while the policy
is applied, I just

00:17:58.200 --> 00:18:01.930
want to point out
a few things here.

00:18:01.930 --> 00:18:03.590
One thing is that you can--

00:18:03.590 --> 00:18:06.150
since your policy and
all of your resources

00:18:06.150 --> 00:18:12.150
are checked into a repo, you can
use your sort of built pipeline

00:18:12.150 --> 00:18:15.810
to make sure that any
changes in the policy

00:18:15.810 --> 00:18:18.260
are validated
through some tests.

00:18:18.260 --> 00:18:20.040
They're reviewed--
whatever process

00:18:20.040 --> 00:18:21.450
you have for the review.

00:18:21.450 --> 00:18:23.160
And then once the
review is complete,

00:18:23.160 --> 00:18:25.350
and you merge those changes
back into the branch

00:18:25.350 --> 00:18:27.420
that you want to
apply to the cluster,

00:18:27.420 --> 00:18:30.780
that Config Management will
automatically deploy it

00:18:30.780 --> 00:18:32.430
in the cluster for you.

00:18:32.430 --> 00:18:34.800
And another great feature
of Config Management

00:18:34.800 --> 00:18:37.740
is that if someone tries
to go in the cluster

00:18:37.740 --> 00:18:39.930
and change some of
those policies--

00:18:39.930 --> 00:18:42.000
they try to change
some of those values--

00:18:42.000 --> 00:18:44.230
Config Management will
detect that change.

00:18:44.230 --> 00:18:48.060
And it will reapply that
change back and make sure

00:18:48.060 --> 00:18:51.480
that the cluster conforms to the
checked in policy in the repo.

00:18:51.480 --> 00:18:55.380
So it will continuously
reconcile the changes

00:18:55.380 --> 00:18:59.460
in the policy with
what's in the repo.

00:18:59.460 --> 00:19:03.040
So let's go back and
see if this has started.

00:19:03.040 --> 00:19:04.973
So as you can see,
it started that.

00:19:04.973 --> 00:19:06.390
And the other thing
that you would

00:19:06.390 --> 00:19:08.640
notice is the hello,
world deployment that I

00:19:08.640 --> 00:19:16.390
had defined in here has been
created in the cluster as well.

00:19:16.390 --> 00:19:19.150
So let's say if you wanted
to change some of those--

00:19:19.150 --> 00:19:24.010
so let's say I want
to change the quota.

00:19:24.010 --> 00:19:25.900
I want to make a change.

00:19:25.900 --> 00:19:30.390
So what I can do
is I can, again,

00:19:30.390 --> 00:19:34.050
follow my workflow for pushing
changes to the git repo.

00:19:45.560 --> 00:19:49.400
So here, I have a change
that I have made to the quota

00:19:49.400 --> 00:19:54.120
that I want to apply to the
repo-- to the cluster as well.

00:19:54.120 --> 00:19:59.005
So what I will do is
I'll add that change.

00:20:02.070 --> 00:20:03.605
And then I will
commit that change.

00:20:15.120 --> 00:20:20.030
And once I push that
change into the repo,

00:20:20.030 --> 00:20:23.060
Config Management will
automatically apply that change

00:20:23.060 --> 00:20:25.220
into the cluster.

00:20:25.220 --> 00:20:33.710
And now if I look at the
quota in that namespace, what

00:20:33.710 --> 00:20:36.890
you will see is that
the quota is 50,

00:20:36.890 --> 00:20:39.990
and it has changed
from 72 down to 50.

00:20:39.990 --> 00:20:44.690
So this is an easy way to make
sure that your policies always

00:20:44.690 --> 00:20:50.390
stay in sync with whatever
you've defined in your repo.

00:20:50.390 --> 00:20:54.840
So getting back-- so there's
a more detailed session,

00:20:54.840 --> 00:20:57.530
where we talk about
the Config Management.

00:20:57.530 --> 00:21:01.320
So you may want to check
out that session as well.

00:21:01.320 --> 00:21:02.930
It's later tomorrow.

00:21:02.930 --> 00:21:05.930
So let's finally
look at how do you

00:21:05.930 --> 00:21:09.830
get to know what your tenants
are up to in the cluster

00:21:09.830 --> 00:21:10.800
as well.

00:21:10.800 --> 00:21:12.470
So for that, we
have a new feature

00:21:12.470 --> 00:21:14.570
in GKE called Usage Metering.

00:21:14.570 --> 00:21:17.540
What it allows you to do is
capture the resource usage

00:21:17.540 --> 00:21:20.430
that your users have
in the namespaces

00:21:20.430 --> 00:21:21.680
that they've been assigned to.

00:21:21.680 --> 00:21:25.550
So you can capture
usage of memory CPU

00:21:25.550 --> 00:21:27.510
and other resources
in the cluster.

00:21:27.510 --> 00:21:29.780
It will put that data
in a BigQuery data

00:21:29.780 --> 00:21:33.630
set that you can later
analyze and visualize as well.

00:21:33.630 --> 00:21:35.780
The important thing is
this does not automatically

00:21:35.780 --> 00:21:37.880
generate the billing
data for those tenants,

00:21:37.880 --> 00:21:41.750
but you can combine that data
with your GCP billing data

00:21:41.750 --> 00:21:44.550
to figure out how to build
your tenants as well.

00:21:44.550 --> 00:21:47.480
And you can visualize
that data using something

00:21:47.480 --> 00:21:51.132
like Data Studio to see
what your tenants are doing.

00:21:51.132 --> 00:21:53.090
So these are just some
of the features that can

00:21:53.090 --> 00:21:55.520
help you set up multi-tenancy.

00:21:55.520 --> 00:21:59.540
But we have a real use case
from Cruise Automation.

00:21:59.540 --> 00:22:02.390
And I'd like to invite Karl
to come up here and walk us

00:22:02.390 --> 00:22:03.770
through their use case.

00:22:08.770 --> 00:22:09.770
KARL ISENBERG: Hey.

00:22:09.770 --> 00:22:12.260
I'm Karl, and I'm
the tech lead manager

00:22:12.260 --> 00:22:14.880
of the PaaS team at Cruise.

00:22:14.880 --> 00:22:18.632
Cruise is doing self-driving
vehicles in San Francisco.

00:22:18.632 --> 00:22:20.090
If you've been
around here a while,

00:22:20.090 --> 00:22:23.450
you've probably seen our cars
driving around San Francisco.

00:22:23.450 --> 00:22:26.450
And we try to connect people
with the places, things,

00:22:26.450 --> 00:22:29.860
and experiences that they
care about doing this.

00:22:29.860 --> 00:22:32.652
This is a visualization from one
of our other open source tools

00:22:32.652 --> 00:22:34.860
that you can find more
information about on our blog.

00:22:34.860 --> 00:22:38.300
But it's basically visualizing
the LiDAR data and our mapping

00:22:38.300 --> 00:22:40.455
data together.

00:22:40.455 --> 00:22:42.080
So one of the problems
we had at Cruise

00:22:42.080 --> 00:22:45.260
when I started probably
a year and a half ago

00:22:45.260 --> 00:22:49.290
is a cluster
proliferation problem.

00:22:49.290 --> 00:22:52.490
So we had a whole bunch of SREs
that were deploying Kubernetes

00:22:52.490 --> 00:22:55.280
clusters on AWS at the time.

00:22:55.280 --> 00:22:58.730
And what we ended up with
was like one cluster per team

00:22:58.730 --> 00:22:59.327
effectively.

00:22:59.327 --> 00:23:00.410
Some of them were sharing.

00:23:00.410 --> 00:23:02.240
Some of them weren't.

00:23:02.240 --> 00:23:05.480
And they were all kind of
special little snowflakes.

00:23:05.480 --> 00:23:06.950
Most of them weren't upgradable.

00:23:06.950 --> 00:23:11.460
Some of them had default, still
configured one-year time bombs

00:23:11.460 --> 00:23:14.840
on the route CA,
which would then

00:23:14.840 --> 00:23:18.210
expire and then lead people
to frantically replacing them.

00:23:18.210 --> 00:23:22.490
So one of the ways we solved
this later was moving to GKE,

00:23:22.490 --> 00:23:27.380
but the way we did immediately
was to start up a PaaS team

00:23:27.380 --> 00:23:33.350
to work on sort of centralizing
the development of a platform

00:23:33.350 --> 00:23:37.070
as a service at Cruise
on top of Kubernetes.

00:23:37.070 --> 00:23:39.200
At Cruise, we have
a lot of workloads

00:23:39.200 --> 00:23:42.480
that are fairly diverse.

00:23:42.480 --> 00:23:46.970
So we do ML and data processing,
backend services, reporting

00:23:46.970 --> 00:23:49.430
batch jobs, productivity
tools, continuous deployment,

00:23:49.430 --> 00:23:52.413
continuous integration,
platform add-ons.

00:23:52.413 --> 00:23:54.330
And a lot of these were
already in containers.

00:23:54.330 --> 00:23:56.450
So we were pretty Cloud native.

00:23:56.450 --> 00:23:59.600
We're just trying to become
more production ready

00:23:59.600 --> 00:24:02.820
before we launch our
services in the coming years.

00:24:02.820 --> 00:24:06.740
So some of the reasons why we
went into multi-tenancy or why

00:24:06.740 --> 00:24:09.710
we decided to invest
in multi-tenancy--

00:24:09.710 --> 00:24:11.420
one was cost savings.

00:24:11.420 --> 00:24:14.270
We already recognized that we
were spending a bunch of money

00:24:14.270 --> 00:24:16.192
on these masters running
Kubernetes clusters.

00:24:16.192 --> 00:24:17.900
And we didn't really
need them because we

00:24:17.900 --> 00:24:20.535
weren't maximizing the masters.

00:24:20.535 --> 00:24:22.160
In our proliferated
clusters, they also

00:24:22.160 --> 00:24:24.080
weren't even highly
available masters.

00:24:24.080 --> 00:24:25.730
So moving to a
central cluster allows

00:24:25.730 --> 00:24:28.490
us to move faster with
that central cluster

00:24:28.490 --> 00:24:31.370
and get it to a more
production ready mature state,

00:24:31.370 --> 00:24:34.340
which helps us towards
our production readiness

00:24:34.340 --> 00:24:38.840
goals of being easier to audit,
having central observability,

00:24:38.840 --> 00:24:41.000
having dashboards
that were mature,

00:24:41.000 --> 00:24:44.060
built-in backups, auto scaling,
all that kind of stuff,

00:24:44.060 --> 00:24:45.740
that if you spread
out to your teams,

00:24:45.740 --> 00:24:49.720
they might not get in the
same pace or even ever.

00:24:49.720 --> 00:24:53.060
And then the other thing we
care about a lot was developer

00:24:53.060 --> 00:24:55.460
productivity or velocity.

00:24:55.460 --> 00:24:57.950
And so, some of this
was around centralizing

00:24:57.950 --> 00:25:01.950
the ingress and egress for
NATs and load balancing.

00:25:01.950 --> 00:25:03.890
And some of it was
around centralizing

00:25:03.890 --> 00:25:05.960
Config Management,
and image building,

00:25:05.960 --> 00:25:07.400
and continuous deployment.

00:25:07.400 --> 00:25:09.920
Basically, all trying
to get our developers

00:25:09.920 --> 00:25:12.290
to have to consider
less of that,

00:25:12.290 --> 00:25:15.440
and also to free up
our SREs so that they

00:25:15.440 --> 00:25:17.690
could focus on
other tasks, rather

00:25:17.690 --> 00:25:20.940
than having to build
the platform themselves.

00:25:20.940 --> 00:25:23.730
So at Cruise, we also have
a hybrid private network,

00:25:23.730 --> 00:25:26.910
which kind of biased a bunch
of our decision making.

00:25:26.910 --> 00:25:29.480
We already are in this
complex multi-cloud world,

00:25:29.480 --> 00:25:32.990
where we have AWS, and GCP,
and on-premise resources.

00:25:32.990 --> 00:25:36.140
And so connecting all this,
thankfully, the platform team

00:25:36.140 --> 00:25:37.490
didn't have to do by ourselves.

00:25:37.490 --> 00:25:40.400
We have a NetEng team
that works with us

00:25:40.400 --> 00:25:43.970
and a good security team that
integrates with all of us

00:25:43.970 --> 00:25:46.552
to sort of build out this
private networking platform.

00:25:46.552 --> 00:25:48.260
But there's a whole
bunch of other pieces

00:25:48.260 --> 00:25:51.380
underneath Kubernetes that
you need to be concerned about

00:25:51.380 --> 00:25:55.040
and make sure that those are
also set up to be multi-tenant.

00:25:55.040 --> 00:25:58.580
One of the ways we did that
was by, as was recommended,

00:25:58.580 --> 00:26:01.340
having separate projects
for separate environments.

00:26:01.340 --> 00:26:04.850
But we also did a
foldering structure

00:26:04.850 --> 00:26:07.490
so that we could completely
isolate our production

00:26:07.490 --> 00:26:13.820
environments and be able to
audit which projects in GCP

00:26:13.820 --> 00:26:15.230
were production projects.

00:26:15.230 --> 00:26:18.140
That way, we could easily
go see what their security

00:26:18.140 --> 00:26:20.840
state was, what the logs were
for the production stuff.

00:26:20.840 --> 00:26:25.052
And mostly security and other
teams that were going and doing

00:26:25.052 --> 00:26:26.510
audits didn't have
to pay attention

00:26:26.510 --> 00:26:28.052
as much to the
development because it

00:26:28.052 --> 00:26:30.540
was completely isolated.

00:26:30.540 --> 00:26:32.720
So we did these
folders, and then we

00:26:32.720 --> 00:26:35.330
have teams that had
a set of projects.

00:26:35.330 --> 00:26:39.350
And then these projects
are in each of the folders.

00:26:39.350 --> 00:26:41.540
There's probably
multiple team folders.

00:26:41.540 --> 00:26:43.130
So one of the
things that get put

00:26:43.130 --> 00:26:44.990
in these folders or
these projects that

00:26:44.990 --> 00:26:47.060
are in the folders
is like NetEng

00:26:47.060 --> 00:26:49.580
manages its own
networking infrastructure

00:26:49.580 --> 00:26:50.725
inside their own projects.

00:26:50.725 --> 00:26:53.100
And then they share that
network with the other projects.

00:26:53.100 --> 00:26:54.650
So none of the
other projects even

00:26:54.650 --> 00:26:57.470
have a default network
local to those projects.

00:26:57.470 --> 00:27:00.530
They just share the one
that's managed centrally.

00:27:00.530 --> 00:27:03.440
And then the teams would want
to use their own SaaS products.

00:27:03.440 --> 00:27:06.020
Sometimes they use
GCE stuff as well.

00:27:06.020 --> 00:27:09.510
So Cloud SQL's a good example
of having your database

00:27:09.510 --> 00:27:11.150
inside of your team's project.

00:27:11.150 --> 00:27:13.080
So only the team has
access to that data.

00:27:13.080 --> 00:27:15.600
The platform team doesn't
need or want access

00:27:15.600 --> 00:27:17.060
to your production data.

00:27:17.060 --> 00:27:18.960
The NetEng probably
doesn't either.

00:27:18.960 --> 00:27:21.210
So it keeps a good
isolation between teams.

00:27:21.210 --> 00:27:24.750
And then this also means that
the PaaS team or the platform

00:27:24.750 --> 00:27:27.360
team can handle the
Kubernetes clusters

00:27:27.360 --> 00:27:29.640
inside their own
project, which means

00:27:29.640 --> 00:27:32.010
that while we have
control of those projects,

00:27:32.010 --> 00:27:33.570
people outside of
the central team

00:27:33.570 --> 00:27:36.080
don't have access to come play
around with the Kubernetes

00:27:36.080 --> 00:27:37.247
settings.

00:27:37.247 --> 00:27:38.830
They just get access
to the Kubernetes

00:27:38.830 --> 00:27:40.650
that's running on it.

00:27:40.650 --> 00:27:44.310
So in Kubernetes in GKE,
we also sort of emulated

00:27:44.310 --> 00:27:46.650
this matrix of multi-tenancies.

00:27:46.650 --> 00:27:50.050
So we have dev staging
and prod shared clusters.

00:27:50.050 --> 00:27:53.770
And we also have namespaces that
are assigned to those teams.

00:27:53.770 --> 00:27:56.910
So this satisfied a
good sort of MVP goal

00:27:56.910 --> 00:27:59.610
of getting everybody
to only be able to see

00:27:59.610 --> 00:28:01.770
what they needed access to.

00:28:01.770 --> 00:28:04.860
So each of them
will run their pods.

00:28:04.860 --> 00:28:06.720
Their pods are isolated
across environments.

00:28:06.720 --> 00:28:09.010
And they're semi-isolated
between teams as well.

00:28:09.010 --> 00:28:13.210
So the teams aren't stepping
on each other's toes.

00:28:13.210 --> 00:28:15.330
So this is just a picture
of some of the pieces

00:28:15.330 --> 00:28:17.142
we put together.

00:28:17.142 --> 00:28:19.350
I think PaaS is a little
bit of a controversial term,

00:28:19.350 --> 00:28:23.470
but in our context, GKE
by itself is not enough.

00:28:23.470 --> 00:28:26.220
We put a whole bunch of
extra work and integrations

00:28:26.220 --> 00:28:29.460
into providing a platform
for our internal tenants.

00:28:29.460 --> 00:28:32.520
So some of that
is GCE components.

00:28:32.520 --> 00:28:34.110
Most of these
little ones in boxes

00:28:34.110 --> 00:28:37.220
are GCP components
or configurations.

00:28:37.220 --> 00:28:41.940
So we have public and private
load balancing routes,

00:28:41.940 --> 00:28:44.250
which are a little bit
different because GCP

00:28:44.250 --> 00:28:48.310
has different functionality
for different features.

00:28:48.310 --> 00:28:52.140
And then we control those
with controllers that are then

00:28:52.140 --> 00:28:54.960
integrating with GKE and
then those controllers either

00:28:54.960 --> 00:29:00.060
have annotations or CRDs that
are in the Kubernetes API

00:29:00.060 --> 00:29:03.540
so that our tenants can
self-service into the selection

00:29:03.540 --> 00:29:04.823
of those features.

00:29:04.823 --> 00:29:06.990
We also have a whole bunch
of security observability

00:29:06.990 --> 00:29:10.290
and deployment functionality
that we provide as a service

00:29:10.290 --> 00:29:12.292
to our internal tenants.

00:29:12.292 --> 00:29:14.250
And that means that they
don't have to reinvent

00:29:14.250 --> 00:29:15.330
the wheel every time.

00:29:15.330 --> 00:29:18.920
And obviously, we didn't just
stamp all this out on day one.

00:29:18.920 --> 00:29:20.420
It was sort of a
growing experience.

00:29:20.420 --> 00:29:23.620
We've been doing this
for over a year now.

00:29:23.620 --> 00:29:27.300
And we moved to GKE
about nine months ago.

00:29:27.300 --> 00:29:30.270
So we took some of it from our
existing deployments and some

00:29:30.270 --> 00:29:31.728
of it, we're still
[INAUDIBLE] out.

00:29:31.728 --> 00:29:34.380
Like the external DNS
up there is a controller

00:29:34.380 --> 00:29:36.640
for managing your DNS records.

00:29:36.640 --> 00:29:38.700
We don't have that
automatic at the moment.

00:29:38.700 --> 00:29:41.760
We're still doing terraforming.

00:29:41.760 --> 00:29:44.460
So Vault integration
is one of the things

00:29:44.460 --> 00:29:48.270
that we did early with help
from our [INAUDIBLE] team.

00:29:48.270 --> 00:29:52.020
And our [INAUDIBLE] team
manages a highly available Vault

00:29:52.020 --> 00:29:52.980
cluster.

00:29:52.980 --> 00:29:57.150
And we decided to use
Vault for secrets,

00:29:57.150 --> 00:29:59.610
instead of just Kubernetes
secrets, primarily

00:29:59.610 --> 00:30:02.490
because it provides a
source of truth separate

00:30:02.490 --> 00:30:04.000
from an individual cluster.

00:30:04.000 --> 00:30:06.570
And this is a concern when
you have multiple clusters.

00:30:06.570 --> 00:30:09.330
If you're small enough that
you can survive on one cluster,

00:30:09.330 --> 00:30:11.163
and you don't want to
have multiple clusters

00:30:11.163 --> 00:30:14.100
per environment, then you can
just use Kubernetes secrets.

00:30:14.100 --> 00:30:16.980
But when you have this
proliferation of clusters,

00:30:16.980 --> 00:30:18.600
you have to start
concerning yourself

00:30:18.600 --> 00:30:20.850
about single source of truth
external to the cluster

00:30:20.850 --> 00:30:22.200
and integrations.

00:30:22.200 --> 00:30:24.370
So this is a fairly
standard pattern.

00:30:24.370 --> 00:30:28.140
Our sort of contribution to
that is a Daytona init container

00:30:28.140 --> 00:30:30.660
that we wrote that
authorizes with Vault

00:30:30.660 --> 00:30:33.522
and downloaded the
secrets using--

00:30:33.522 --> 00:30:34.230
let me step back.

00:30:34.230 --> 00:30:38.160
It logs into Vault using the
service account that's injected

00:30:38.160 --> 00:30:39.490
into your Kubernetes pod.

00:30:39.490 --> 00:30:41.580
So that's a default
Kubernetes feature,

00:30:41.580 --> 00:30:44.950
and then a Vault configuration
feature that's baked in.

00:30:44.950 --> 00:30:47.305
And then Daytona we added
that does sort of the glue

00:30:47.305 --> 00:30:48.180
to stick it together.

00:30:48.180 --> 00:30:49.530
It uses it in-memory volume.

00:30:49.530 --> 00:30:51.920
That's to make sure it doesn't
write anything to disk,

00:30:51.920 --> 00:30:53.337
and it won't get
persisted outside

00:30:53.337 --> 00:30:54.780
of the lifecycle of your pod.

00:30:54.780 --> 00:30:58.320
And then it slurps it in through
an app container, volume mount.

00:30:58.320 --> 00:31:00.990
So that you can either read
it from your application,

00:31:00.990 --> 00:31:03.090
or your application
can have an entry point

00:31:03.090 --> 00:31:08.910
script that converts from a
file to an environment variable.

00:31:08.910 --> 00:31:12.990
So another thing we did is GCP
service account integration

00:31:12.990 --> 00:31:16.560
through Vault. So
as was mentioned,

00:31:16.560 --> 00:31:19.110
the pod identity feature
was not available

00:31:19.110 --> 00:31:21.000
when we started doing this.

00:31:21.000 --> 00:31:23.010
And so one of the things
this allows us to do

00:31:23.010 --> 00:31:26.340
is provide the pods
with their own identity

00:31:26.340 --> 00:31:29.850
from Vault using a
GCP service account.

00:31:29.850 --> 00:31:32.160
So that means that we can
authorize these things

00:31:32.160 --> 00:31:33.870
before the pod exists.

00:31:33.870 --> 00:31:37.830
And the pod can go out
and perform GCP actions.

00:31:37.830 --> 00:31:41.860
And probably the primary one
is talking to the database.

00:31:41.860 --> 00:31:45.330
So they use that with a sidecar
proxy to talk to Cloud SQL,

00:31:45.330 --> 00:31:45.960
for example.

00:31:48.830 --> 00:31:52.780
So once you get into this sort
of central managing a Vault,

00:31:52.780 --> 00:31:55.700
you end up having to manage
your namespaces in Vault

00:31:55.700 --> 00:31:56.700
a little more carefully.

00:31:56.700 --> 00:31:58.575
You can't just have
everybody writing secrets

00:31:58.575 --> 00:31:59.840
wherever they want.

00:31:59.840 --> 00:32:03.220
So what we went with
was some prefix that's

00:32:03.220 --> 00:32:08.140
specific to us and then sort
of a namespacing pattern that

00:32:08.140 --> 00:32:11.200
allows for isolating
the environments,

00:32:11.200 --> 00:32:13.450
and the namespace, and
the application secrets,

00:32:13.450 --> 00:32:16.450
which means that I
can say this team

00:32:16.450 --> 00:32:19.630
has permission to write
to all of the environment

00:32:19.630 --> 00:32:21.490
secrets for all of
the applications

00:32:21.490 --> 00:32:23.500
under their namespace.

00:32:23.500 --> 00:32:25.090
But other teams
don't have access

00:32:25.090 --> 00:32:27.670
to even see or list those.

00:32:27.670 --> 00:32:29.710
And then the
application itself only

00:32:29.710 --> 00:32:32.620
has access to see the
secrets for its environment

00:32:32.620 --> 00:32:35.710
for that specific app
for that namespace.

00:32:35.710 --> 00:32:39.070
And that allows us to granularly
control permissions in Vault.

00:32:39.070 --> 00:32:42.310
And we ended up doing sort
of [INAUDIBLE] workflow

00:32:42.310 --> 00:32:46.960
to provision Vault configuration
in order to enable that.

00:32:46.960 --> 00:32:49.150
Another thing we did was
group our own bindings.

00:32:49.150 --> 00:32:52.540
So we open sourced it
about six months ago.

00:32:52.540 --> 00:32:58.000
And this was to make up for
the lack of role binding, which

00:32:58.000 --> 00:33:01.660
is a beta feature now
for Google Groups.

00:33:01.660 --> 00:33:03.680
But it didn't used to exist.

00:33:03.680 --> 00:33:06.050
And we built in a couple
more features into this.

00:33:06.050 --> 00:33:08.920
So one of the things that
was explained earlier

00:33:08.920 --> 00:33:13.770
is that you can't necessarily
use the project level

00:33:13.770 --> 00:33:16.190
roles in order to bind
at the namespace level.

00:33:16.190 --> 00:33:18.790
So what we had to do was
build out namespace admin,

00:33:18.790 --> 00:33:21.010
namespace editor,
and namespace viewer,

00:33:21.010 --> 00:33:23.020
and give those to
individual groups.

00:33:23.020 --> 00:33:25.030
So we map those to
groups, and the team

00:33:25.030 --> 00:33:27.100
gets that group membership.

00:33:27.100 --> 00:33:29.320
That means I can have
our Google Group,

00:33:29.320 --> 00:33:30.940
and there's some
manager that owns it.

00:33:30.940 --> 00:33:33.100
And the manager just goes,
adds new team members

00:33:33.100 --> 00:33:35.140
or removes team
members that leaves.

00:33:35.140 --> 00:33:37.660
And then they don't have
to perform a role binding

00:33:37.660 --> 00:33:38.720
manually.

00:33:38.720 --> 00:33:40.960
This RBAC sync functionality
is a controller

00:33:40.960 --> 00:33:44.130
that will watch a
configuration in a CRD.

00:33:44.130 --> 00:33:47.680
And the CRD says this
group gets this permission,

00:33:47.680 --> 00:33:50.170
and so then it will map
that out to the bindings

00:33:50.170 --> 00:33:52.210
by reading through Google
Groups to figure out

00:33:52.210 --> 00:33:53.920
who's in the group.

00:33:53.920 --> 00:33:55.960
Another functionality
that we stuffed into this

00:33:55.960 --> 00:33:58.240
was the ability to put
service accounts in groups

00:33:58.240 --> 00:34:01.540
because currently, Google
Groups don't support that.

00:34:01.540 --> 00:34:04.960
So what that means is
that we can give a service

00:34:04.960 --> 00:34:08.207
account a specific role
and then add it to a group

00:34:08.207 --> 00:34:09.790
whenever we make new
service accounts.

00:34:09.790 --> 00:34:11.469
And we can use GCP
service accounts

00:34:11.469 --> 00:34:15.889
to do that, rather than just
Kubernetes service accounts.

00:34:15.889 --> 00:34:17.650
So there's a couple
of things that

00:34:17.650 --> 00:34:22.810
are sort of unsolved or bleeding
edge problems that we're still

00:34:22.810 --> 00:34:24.139
kind of trying to work on.

00:34:24.139 --> 00:34:26.080
So one of those is
Kubernetes operators

00:34:26.080 --> 00:34:27.159
in a tenancy environment.

00:34:27.159 --> 00:34:30.909
The CRDs are global
definitions in Kubernetes,

00:34:30.909 --> 00:34:35.679
so you can't necessarily
allow your namespace tenants

00:34:35.679 --> 00:34:39.100
to create CRD definitions.

00:34:39.100 --> 00:34:42.820
So what we do is we, as
a cluster operating team,

00:34:42.820 --> 00:34:47.889
deploy CRD definitions and then
allow them to make CRD resource

00:34:47.889 --> 00:34:49.429
objects in their namespace.

00:34:49.429 --> 00:34:53.320
But that means you can only
have one sort of permutation

00:34:53.320 --> 00:34:55.360
of that CRD at a time
because CRDs weren't

00:34:55.360 --> 00:34:57.695
designed for multi-tenancy.

00:34:57.695 --> 00:34:59.320
So that means we're
effectively helping

00:34:59.320 --> 00:35:00.940
people deploy operators.

00:35:00.940 --> 00:35:04.540
Also, the Container
Registry is a tenant.

00:35:04.540 --> 00:35:07.570
Tenant dies at the
level of Google

00:35:07.570 --> 00:35:09.770
projects, or GCP
projects, rather.

00:35:09.770 --> 00:35:13.390
So if you want full
isolation between teams,

00:35:13.390 --> 00:35:16.630
every team has to
manage their own GCR.

00:35:16.630 --> 00:35:19.300
Otherwise, what we do is we host
a central one for development

00:35:19.300 --> 00:35:21.710
purposes and give
everybody like admin to it.

00:35:21.710 --> 00:35:25.810
That means they have push
and delete functionalities.

00:35:25.810 --> 00:35:27.910
And then everybody
can push to that,

00:35:27.910 --> 00:35:31.310
but we don't allow pulling
from that for the production

00:35:31.310 --> 00:35:31.810
cluster.

00:35:31.810 --> 00:35:33.700
So our production
cluster, we only

00:35:33.700 --> 00:35:35.830
give access to service accounts.

00:35:35.830 --> 00:35:37.390
And those service
accounts are then

00:35:37.390 --> 00:35:40.670
used in continuous
integration or building.

00:35:40.670 --> 00:35:42.100
So all of the
containers that run

00:35:42.100 --> 00:35:47.140
on our production environment
we know were pushed from CI.

00:35:47.140 --> 00:35:50.260
Stackdriver Logging
also is tenantized

00:35:50.260 --> 00:35:51.560
at the project level.

00:35:51.560 --> 00:35:53.860
And because you have
a shared cluster,

00:35:53.860 --> 00:35:57.070
that means that all of the logs
from all of your containers

00:35:57.070 --> 00:35:59.445
are admitted to the same
shared project, which

00:35:59.445 --> 00:36:00.820
creates a little
bit of a problem

00:36:00.820 --> 00:36:03.342
because we've only given people
view access to that project.

00:36:03.342 --> 00:36:05.050
We didn't want to give
them editor access

00:36:05.050 --> 00:36:07.240
to step on each
other's toes, which

00:36:07.240 --> 00:36:10.120
means they can't make
log to metric conversions

00:36:10.120 --> 00:36:11.650
or log dashboards.

00:36:11.650 --> 00:36:13.540
So in order to
enable that, we had

00:36:13.540 --> 00:36:19.240
to build a fan out system
that will take [INAUDIBLE]

00:36:19.240 --> 00:36:22.810
Stackdriver and admits
to other projects

00:36:22.810 --> 00:36:26.380
from specific namespaces.

00:36:26.380 --> 00:36:28.900
DataDog metrics-- we use
DataDog instead of Stackdriver

00:36:28.900 --> 00:36:30.730
for our metrics right now.

00:36:30.730 --> 00:36:33.290
And primarily, that allows
us to aggregate them all.

00:36:33.290 --> 00:36:36.550
But it's also not
super multi-tenant.

00:36:36.550 --> 00:36:39.610
So you might have both a
fan out and a fan in problem

00:36:39.610 --> 00:36:41.530
with both logging and metrics.

00:36:41.530 --> 00:36:44.045
Because your auditors might
want to see all of them,

00:36:44.045 --> 00:36:46.420
and your teams just want to
see the ones they care about.

00:36:46.420 --> 00:36:51.740
So GCP has the ability to slurp
all your logs into one auditing

00:36:51.740 --> 00:36:52.280
project.

00:36:52.280 --> 00:36:54.980
So we use that for auditing,
and then we use this other fan

00:36:54.980 --> 00:36:57.680
out solution for the tenants.

00:36:57.680 --> 00:37:02.180
Spinnaker's another one where
we had to build our own operator

00:37:02.180 --> 00:37:04.760
to make Spinnaker more isolated.

00:37:04.760 --> 00:37:06.470
Because we have so
many namespaces--

00:37:06.470 --> 00:37:10.220
we have 20 namespaces per
cluster and maybe a dozen

00:37:10.220 --> 00:37:11.270
clusters--

00:37:11.270 --> 00:37:14.000
that means that we are doing
account binding and role

00:37:14.000 --> 00:37:16.010
binding in the
Spinnaker to isolate

00:37:16.010 --> 00:37:18.350
who can deploy to
what from Spinnaker,

00:37:18.350 --> 00:37:19.880
which is a little complicated.

00:37:19.880 --> 00:37:22.010
And then on resource
isolation, probably one

00:37:22.010 --> 00:37:25.700
of the weaknesses of Kubernetes
in general for multi-tenancy

00:37:25.700 --> 00:37:28.490
is that there are some things
that are not well isolated yet.

00:37:28.490 --> 00:37:30.120
So CPU and memory are great.

00:37:30.120 --> 00:37:31.580
They're isolated
you opt into it,

00:37:31.580 --> 00:37:34.520
and then you have that
amount of resource.

00:37:34.520 --> 00:37:36.170
It even has preemption on those.

00:37:36.170 --> 00:37:39.890
But local disk space, there is
a beta feature in Kubernetes

00:37:39.890 --> 00:37:41.690
that isn't in GKE yet.

00:37:41.690 --> 00:37:43.460
So we can't isolate that yet.

00:37:43.460 --> 00:37:46.310
Disk I/O is completely on
isolated in Kubernetes,

00:37:46.310 --> 00:37:48.380
so if you are on spinning
hard drives, which

00:37:48.380 --> 00:37:50.450
I don't recommend,
you can actually

00:37:50.450 --> 00:37:53.030
knock over the Docker
daemon if you use

00:37:53.030 --> 00:37:54.470
too much disk at the same time.

00:37:54.470 --> 00:37:57.380
So we switched to SSDs,
and that has mostly

00:37:57.380 --> 00:37:58.400
eliminated the problem.

00:37:58.400 --> 00:38:00.380
But it's still unisolated.

00:38:00.380 --> 00:38:01.640
Ingress and egress--

00:38:01.640 --> 00:38:05.420
I mentioned us having solved
those sort of at the platform

00:38:05.420 --> 00:38:08.100
level, but they're also
a little bit unisolated.

00:38:08.100 --> 00:38:09.590
One of the ways
we're hoping to get

00:38:09.590 --> 00:38:14.370
towards isolation in the
future here is with Istio.

00:38:14.370 --> 00:38:17.460
So Istio adds the
sidecars to each container

00:38:17.460 --> 00:38:19.460
and allows you to monitor
the ingress and egress

00:38:19.460 --> 00:38:21.350
from every container,
which allows you to do

00:38:21.350 --> 00:38:22.940
sort of traffic management.

00:38:22.940 --> 00:38:25.813
But Kubernetes doesn't
come with that by default.

00:38:25.813 --> 00:38:27.230
So there's a whole
bunch of things

00:38:27.230 --> 00:38:30.920
we also want to do that we
haven't done just to sort of be

00:38:30.920 --> 00:38:34.250
an example of all of the things
that you might want to consider

00:38:34.250 --> 00:38:36.020
when you're building
not just a Kubernetes

00:38:36.020 --> 00:38:39.440
cluster, but a multi-tenant,
multi-environment, private

00:38:39.440 --> 00:38:43.460
cloud hybrid network cluster.

00:38:43.460 --> 00:38:45.710
So one of the things
I would recommend

00:38:45.710 --> 00:38:48.080
is to put in your
defaults and quotas

00:38:48.080 --> 00:38:49.665
before you put
tenants on your system

00:38:49.665 --> 00:38:52.040
because what we've learned is
now that we've gone and had

00:38:52.040 --> 00:38:53.990
people on our systems,
we can't add quotas

00:38:53.990 --> 00:38:55.640
without breaking them.

00:38:55.640 --> 00:38:58.190
So we have to do an analysis
on who's using what,

00:38:58.190 --> 00:39:01.070
and then make them
per namespace quotas,

00:39:01.070 --> 00:39:03.410
and then add a GitOps
approval process and all

00:39:03.410 --> 00:39:04.578
that kind of stuff.

00:39:04.578 --> 00:39:05.870
So that's a little complicated.

00:39:05.870 --> 00:39:07.475
Do that upfront.

00:39:07.475 --> 00:39:09.350
We would also like to
go to project isolation

00:39:09.350 --> 00:39:10.820
instead of team isolation.

00:39:10.820 --> 00:39:13.850
And what I mean by that is
reducing the blast radius

00:39:13.850 --> 00:39:15.600
of a compromised project.

00:39:15.600 --> 00:39:17.600
So if someone comes
in through a web app

00:39:17.600 --> 00:39:20.600
API or something
compromises a web app,

00:39:20.600 --> 00:39:22.910
and then they have
control over the container

00:39:22.910 --> 00:39:25.580
for whatever one weakness
in your application,

00:39:25.580 --> 00:39:27.890
you don't want it to
be on the same network

00:39:27.890 --> 00:39:30.680
in the same namespace
with everything

00:39:30.680 --> 00:39:31.807
else that team is running.

00:39:31.807 --> 00:39:33.640
You want to sort of
reduce the blast radius.

00:39:33.640 --> 00:39:35.730
So there's a bunch of
different ways to do that.

00:39:35.730 --> 00:39:38.480
One of them is with by
having smaller namespaces,

00:39:38.480 --> 00:39:42.500
and another one is by doing
this sort of network policy

00:39:42.500 --> 00:39:45.230
that restricts who
has access to what.

00:39:45.230 --> 00:39:47.420
Another one is with
pod security policy

00:39:47.420 --> 00:39:51.180
with reducing what the
pods can actually do.

00:39:51.180 --> 00:39:55.970
And then a couple of these other
functionalities-- web hooks

00:39:55.970 --> 00:39:59.180
have been talked a lot about
a bunch in the Kubernetes

00:39:59.180 --> 00:40:02.030
environment or
ecosystem for sort

00:40:02.030 --> 00:40:04.670
of restraining or
enforcing what people

00:40:04.670 --> 00:40:06.800
can apply to your cluster.

00:40:06.800 --> 00:40:10.450
But there's no sort of off
the shelf solution for that.

00:40:10.450 --> 00:40:17.060
There's OPA, which is a policy
sort of administrator that

00:40:17.060 --> 00:40:18.920
can do some web hooks
for you, but there's

00:40:18.920 --> 00:40:21.540
no built-in policies that
you can just slap in place.

00:40:21.540 --> 00:40:24.500
So there's some work
to be done there.

00:40:24.500 --> 00:40:26.750
And then we would also like
an automated build system.

00:40:26.750 --> 00:40:29.870
I'd really love a Docker
hub automated build

00:40:29.870 --> 00:40:31.550
for on-premise
behind my firewall,

00:40:31.550 --> 00:40:32.910
but it doesn't really exist.

00:40:32.910 --> 00:40:35.570
So there's a couple of
different products you can use.

00:40:35.570 --> 00:40:37.820
Cloud Builder is one of them.

00:40:37.820 --> 00:40:40.238
Knative Build is another one.

00:40:40.238 --> 00:40:41.780
So we're going to
be working on that.

00:40:41.780 --> 00:40:45.440
Microsegmentation of the network
also reduces blast radius.

00:40:45.440 --> 00:40:47.630
Security would love
us to get to that.

00:40:47.630 --> 00:40:50.270
We're hoping to get there with
Istio and network policies.

00:40:50.270 --> 00:40:52.200
And usage media metering
was talked about.

00:40:52.200 --> 00:40:52.700
It's great.

00:40:52.700 --> 00:40:56.060
We're going to get there,
but because it is still beta,

00:40:56.060 --> 00:40:57.890
we haven't played with it.

00:40:57.890 --> 00:41:00.680
And then service
RBAC is also kind of

00:41:00.680 --> 00:41:05.120
important with your users to
services or user to Kubernetes.

00:41:05.120 --> 00:41:06.950
That's built in with GKE.

00:41:06.950 --> 00:41:10.130
You get your Google
user auth, and then you

00:41:10.130 --> 00:41:12.360
can do role binding
against users.

00:41:12.360 --> 00:41:14.700
But once your
application is running,

00:41:14.700 --> 00:41:16.910
how do your applications
authorize with each other?

00:41:16.910 --> 00:41:19.640
And this is kind of a problem
everybody has to solve.

00:41:19.640 --> 00:41:23.780
And there's no out of the box
solution that I know of yet.

00:41:23.780 --> 00:41:25.310
End to end TLS is another one.

00:41:25.310 --> 00:41:28.760
GKE doesn't come
with this built in.

00:41:28.760 --> 00:41:31.970
You can use sort of a let's
encrypt controller solution.

00:41:31.970 --> 00:41:33.470
But we wanted one
that would allow

00:41:33.470 --> 00:41:36.500
us to do both public
and private domains.

00:41:36.500 --> 00:41:39.170
So then we need to
host our own CA,

00:41:39.170 --> 00:41:40.820
and automate the
controller for it,

00:41:40.820 --> 00:41:43.538
and then make it different
from the public one,

00:41:43.538 --> 00:41:44.330
that kind of thing.

00:41:44.330 --> 00:41:47.220
Plus, we don't really want to
be inserting TLS certificates

00:41:47.220 --> 00:41:49.650
into every pod.

00:41:49.650 --> 00:41:51.930
We'd rather inject
to the load balancer,

00:41:51.930 --> 00:41:54.900
and then have Service
Mesh handle the last mile

00:41:54.900 --> 00:41:55.710
encryption.

00:41:55.710 --> 00:41:58.470
That way, we're
not putting secrets

00:41:58.470 --> 00:42:00.393
that can be compromised
down into the pods.

00:42:00.393 --> 00:42:02.310
We have sort of a two-layer
encryption scheme.

00:42:04.960 --> 00:42:07.470
So basically,
moral of the story,

00:42:07.470 --> 00:42:10.140
if you're big enough to
build a platform here

00:42:10.140 --> 00:42:12.510
on top of Kubernetes,
which Kubernetes

00:42:12.510 --> 00:42:14.400
is a platform for
building platforms,

00:42:14.400 --> 00:42:17.160
then you're probably big
enough to make it multi-tenant.

00:42:17.160 --> 00:42:19.650
Multi-tenancy does
require additional work

00:42:19.650 --> 00:42:20.700
on top of Kubernetes.

00:42:20.700 --> 00:42:23.680
And I wouldn't necessarily
say it's for everyone.

00:42:23.680 --> 00:42:28.470
But if your alternative
is multi-environment or

00:42:28.470 --> 00:42:30.060
multi-instance,
then you're going

00:42:30.060 --> 00:42:32.435
to have to build some layer
underneath it that allows you

00:42:32.435 --> 00:42:34.460
to deploy a bunch of clusters.

00:42:34.460 --> 00:42:36.210
And so if you're already
building a layer,

00:42:36.210 --> 00:42:39.580
you might as well make
it multi-tenant instead.

00:42:39.580 --> 00:42:41.880
So there's obviously
tradeoffs and pros and cons,

00:42:41.880 --> 00:42:44.625
so take it with a grain of
salt. But that's our experience.

00:42:44.625 --> 00:42:46.500
And I think we've got
it working pretty well.

00:42:46.500 --> 00:42:50.400
If you go in a car in Cruise or
you see those driving around,

00:42:50.400 --> 00:42:51.870
those are all
talking to our cloud

00:42:51.870 --> 00:42:54.972
with the backend running
on multi-tenant Kubernetes.

00:42:54.972 --> 00:42:59.630
[APPLAUSE]

00:42:59.630 --> 00:43:00.630
SHAHID MASUD: All right.

00:43:00.630 --> 00:43:03.150
Thank you, Karl, for
sharing the journey

00:43:03.150 --> 00:43:06.210
of Cruise Automation
to using Kubernetes

00:43:06.210 --> 00:43:07.990
in a multi-tenant fashion.

00:43:07.990 --> 00:43:12.300
I just want to close out with
a few key sort of takeaways.

00:43:12.300 --> 00:43:16.290
So by centralizing,
Kubernetes has

00:43:16.290 --> 00:43:19.620
a lot of learning that you
have to do to understand

00:43:19.620 --> 00:43:21.210
how to run it well.

00:43:21.210 --> 00:43:23.850
And by specializing that
knowledge with one team

00:43:23.850 --> 00:43:28.650
that operates your clusters not
only builds that sort of center

00:43:28.650 --> 00:43:31.620
of excellence, which allows
you to implement the best

00:43:31.620 --> 00:43:33.900
practices across
multiple clusters,

00:43:33.900 --> 00:43:35.970
but it also frees
up your developers

00:43:35.970 --> 00:43:38.730
to spend their time on
developing stuff that

00:43:38.730 --> 00:43:40.360
adds value to your company.

00:43:40.360 --> 00:43:42.630
So I think that's some
of the key takeaways.

00:43:42.630 --> 00:43:45.480
And then it gives you the
operational efficiency

00:43:45.480 --> 00:43:49.800
by letting that
specialized team to manage

00:43:49.800 --> 00:43:51.850
those clusters for you.

00:43:51.850 --> 00:43:54.090
And it also saves you
some infrastructure cost

00:43:54.090 --> 00:43:58.300
by consolidating a large
number of clusters into fewer

00:43:58.300 --> 00:44:00.360
multi-tenant clusters as well.

00:44:00.360 --> 00:44:02.340
And GKE has some
new features that

00:44:02.340 --> 00:44:04.620
can help you set up
that multi-tenancy.

00:44:04.620 --> 00:44:06.720
As we heard from
Karl and Cruise,

00:44:06.720 --> 00:44:08.250
there's still some
work to be done.

00:44:08.250 --> 00:44:11.400
And you'll see those
features come out as well.

00:44:11.400 --> 00:44:15.650
[MUSIC PLAYING]

